Objective
Learn how to investigate Windows Firewall profiles and rules, then trace a firewall rule to the associated port, service, process, and PID.
1. Firewall Profile Check
Command:
Get-NetFirewallProfile
Key findings:
Domain profile: Enabled
Private profile: Enabled
Some individual settings showed NotConfigured
Lesson: NotConfigured does not mean the firewall is disabled.
2. Firewall Rule Investigation
Command:
Get-NetFirewallRule | Where-Object {$_.Enabled -eq "True"} | Select-Object -First 10 DisplayName, Direction, Action
Investigated:
mDNS (UDP-Out)
Direction: Outbound
Action: Allow
Protocol: UDP
Remote port: 5353
Program: svchost.exe
Service: Dnscache
Conclusion: The rule is consistent with legitimate Windows networking/DNS-related activity.
3. Inbound Firewall Investigation
Investigated:
Remote Assistance (DCOM-In)
Findings:
Direction: Inbound
Action: Allow
Protocol: TCP
Local port: 135
Service: RPCSS
State: Running
PID: 500
Executable: C:\Windows\System32\svchost.exe
4. PID Verification
Command:
netstat -ano | findstr ":135"
Result:
TCP    0.0.0.0:135    0.0.0.0:0    LISTENING    500
TCP    [::]:135       [::]:0       LISTENING    500
Important finding: The PID from RPCSS (500) matches the PID listening on TCP 135 (500).
